Shares of Voltas rose as much as 2.8% to an intraday high of Rs 1,131.40 per share on September 23 a day after the company's analyst meet.
The company held an analyst meeting Tuesday in which it highlighted prioritising the company's market share in the unitary cooling products segment and profit growth over margin expansion. For the September quarter, the company sees 15–20% on-year growth in secondary sales for the industry, brokerages said, citing the company's management. This is due to El Nino and a favourable base.
HSBC has a 'Buy' rating on the stock and target price of Rs 1,450.
"Market share improved and lead over second-largest player widened. Stock inventory holding low," said HSBC.
Jefferies has a 'Buy' rating on the stock with target price of Rs 1,495.
"MD and CFO reiterated Voltas' focus on sales (volumes). Market share rose to 18.6% in July26 (15.9% in FY26). Channel inventory is normal at 30 days now. Secondary sales in September quarter have been healthy so far (est 15-20%) due to low base. But despite total price hike of 12% YTD, cost surge continues due to Middle East conflict (commodities, weak INR). Thus, factor margin pressure and cut EPS," said Jefferies.
"Management is constructive on the long-term RAC opportunity, supported by low penetration and rising incomes, but expects competition to intensify. It is not targeting a specific margin milestone with a fixed date; rather, the expectation is for margin to improve gradually as commodity inflation
moderates, pricing actions flow through and operating leverage improves. In the near term, VOLT focuses on market share retention and absolute profitability, with distribution, service, product quality, pricing discipline and brand strength remaining critical," said Motilal Oswal Financial Services.
The brokerage has a 'Neutral' rating on the stock with target price of Rs 1,170.
On September 23, Voltas shares closed 2.6% higher at Rs 1,129.3 apiece.
