The benchmark indices reversed some of their recent gains, with the Nifty 50 falling 0.43 percent on September 22. Market breadth favoured the bears, with about 1,805 shares declining against 1,448 advancing shares on the NSE. Range-bound trading may continue until the market decisively breaks above or below the high-low range of September 15. Here are some short-term trading ideas to consider:
 Rajesh Dashrath Bhosale, Fund Manager - Advisory at Renaissance 
 Star Health and Allied Insurance Company | CMP: Rs 544.6 
On the weekly timeframe, Star Health remains in a bullish structure, forming higher tops and higher bottoms since the April 2025 low near Rs 340. After breaking above Rs 535 in July 2026 on strong volumes, the stock rallied towards Rs 625 and has now retraced to the Rs 535–545 breakout zone.
The stock is showing a bullish reversal from the previous breakout levels, supported by the rising 20-week EMA, while corrective volumes remain subdued, indicating healthy profit booking. The weekly RSI has cooled to the 50–55 zone and is now turning upward, supporting renewed momentum.
Usha Martin has witnessed a vertical rally over the last week, breaking above the stiff Rs 520 resistance level, which had capped the upside multiple times. The breakout confirms a strong bullish range breakout, with prices entering uncharted territory, indicating clear dominance of buyers.
The breakout is visible across major timeframes and is supported by strong volumes, suggesting that positive momentum and outperformance are likely to sustain in the near term.
Carborundum Universal staged a sharp rally in mid-June from around Rs 950 to Rs 1,300, followed by nearly three months of consolidation between Rs 1,020 and Rs 1,192. The recent breakout above Rs 1,192, backed by the highest volume since the June rally, confirms a strong range breakout and fresh demand.
The Rs 1,190–1,195 zone has now turned into a key support area, indicating a positive polarity shift and scope for further upside.
 Ashish Kyal, Founder and CEO of Waves Strategy Advisors 
In the previous session, Engineers India rallied more than 6 percent, decisively surpassing the previous swing high near Rs 290 and closing above this level, indicating a positive shift in the price structure. The stock has been following a 29-period Time Cycle, which has now turned bullish as prices broke above Rs 290, indicating improving momentum.
Recently, the stock took support at the 40-period EMA near the Rs 253 level, which also coincides with the 38.2 percent Fibonacci retracement level. Post this support, the stock witnessed a sharp upside move, highlighting its significance. The ADX is also trading near the 39 level, suggesting sustained momentum.
For now, any dip towards Rs 295–298 can be used as an opportunity to ride the ongoing trend, with key support around Rs 280. The targets are placed at Rs 310, followed by Rs 325.
 Lumax Auto Technologies | CMP: Rs 2,116.4 
On the daily chart, Lumax Auto Technologies has been consolidating within a 220-point range of Rs 1,895–2,115 since August 12. The stock is now trading near the upper end of this range and appears to be on the verge of a potential breakout. The stock has also managed to protect its lows on a closing basis since September 17, indicating sustained buying interest at lower levels.
Meanwhile, the Bollinger Bands have started expanding, signalling an increase in volatility and the possibility of a larger price move. For now, a sustained move above Rs 2,130 could confirm a breakout from the consolidation range and trigger fresh upside momentum towards Rs 2,200, followed by Rs 2,280, with Rs 2,060 acting as an important support level.
In the previous session, RBL Bank outperformed the broader market, rallying more than 5 percent and closing near a fresh 52-week high of Rs 424.40, indicating strong buying momentum. Earlier this week, the stock found support near the 20 EMA, which coincided with the 30-period Time Cycle zone. The sharp reversal from this support triggered a strong upside move, with the cycle now turning to the buy side.
Following the sharp rise, traders should avoid chasing the stock at higher levels and instead look for buying opportunities on dips to participate in the ongoing trend, which is in the form of Wave 3 of (3), with potential upside levels of Rs 435–445. The bullish view remains valid as long as Rs 400 is protected on the downside.
 Amol Athawale, VP Technical Research at Kotak Securities 
